Bug #004: Browser page script fails on startup from an escaped newline in JavaScript
Date: 2026-10-04
Module / Area: Browser app: page initialization / test log polling

Cause:
A JavaScript string inside the HTML template used `"\n"` in Python, but because the template is a Python triple-quoted string, the backslash sequence was processed by Python before the browser read it. This turned `"\n"` into a literal line break inside the JavaScript string, which caused a syntax error and stopped the rest of the page script from running.

Symptoms:
- HTML page loads, but the catalog stays empty
- Metrics stay at "—"
- Browser console shows a JavaScript syntax error at the test log code
- The app looks unresponsive even though the page is rendered

Solution:
1. Escape the backslash as `\\n` inside the Python template string so the browser receives `\n` as intended.
2. Keep the rest of the browser script guard-safe and only run the catalog loader after the DOM is ready.
3. Validate the rendered page in a browser-like DOM environment before shipping.

Prevention:
- Be careful with escaping when embedding JavaScript inside Python strings.
- Always test generated HTML with a browser-like runtime when the page script is complex.
- Keep a record of string-escape bugs in this log to avoid repeating them.

